WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.063
As they say, back to office.

2
00:00:02.063 --> 00:00:10.100
In my case that's a return to my practical kitchen when it comes to filming content for this YouTube channel.

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.660
Today I have a special topic that, I believe, will be useful to you,

4
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:19.325
and so each time I decide to start from the very beginning, so let's go

5
00:00:19.325 --> 00:00:23.800
step by step together, because I know how much this can mean.

6
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.330
Today I'm having guests over:

7
00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.920
two adults and two children who are already at an age when they can eat everything,

8
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:37.500
but still I can't make them anything very spicy and heavy with lots of vegetables and the like.

9
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.817
I decided to make a TikTok recipe today that has been circling in my head for a long time.

10
00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:49.900
It's a recipe with ground meat that can be served in such a way

11
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:57.222
that I don't set a regular table, and the kitchen counter turns into a buffet,

12
00:00:57.222 --> 00:01:01.300
and we'll basically make my flatbreads,

13
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:10.711
then you'll see how the meat is placed next to each other, and we put out lots of sides with loads of little things,

14
00:01:10.711 --> 00:01:13.400
where everyone can make themselves

15
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.894
something like a gyros, but it's not a gyros, because it's ground meat, but rather, say,

16
00:01:17.894 --> 00:01:20.908
an upgraded kebab, I don't know what to call it.

17
00:01:20.908 --> 00:01:23.100
So I think you'll find it interesting

18
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:31.815
if in the near future you want to take on cooking for some event you're hosting at home.

19
00:01:31.815 --> 00:01:33.500
So, step by step,

20
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.000
let's see how I receive my brother and his two children today.

21
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:43.300
Let's roll up our sleeves and first take flour and yogurt, we'll need them for the flatbreads.

22
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:53.000
So, I'll need this big bowl. I'll need yogurt, and I'm going to the pantry for flour.

23
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.300
Here's the yogurt and here's the flour.

24
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:05.002
So, I have my own little measure for this, which gives four tortillas,

25
00:02:05.002 --> 00:02:09.900
and that takes roughly 350 grams of flour and 300 milliliters of yogurt.

26
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:19.500
I'll make it now from a kilo of flour and add yogurt as much as needed, I already know what the thickness should be.

27
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:24.459
When you make them, basically... But, as I said,

28
00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:26.623
I'll make it, and I'll write the basic measure below,

29
00:02:26.623 --> 00:02:31.800
and you basically scale it depending on the number of people.

30
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:36.826
In any case, it's made very simply: flour, yogurt,

31
00:02:36.826 --> 00:02:39.888
baking powder and a bit of salt. That's all,

32
00:02:39.888 --> 00:02:44.600
knead the dough, and you don't need to wait for it to rise, which is great.

33
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:55.529
I'll put in a little more than half of the baking powder. I think I can actually put in all of it,

34
00:02:55.529 --> 00:02:58.700
considering that the flour is a kilo.

35
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:11.500
I'll just mix it a little and literally add yogurt to the right limit, until I get a nicely formed dough,

36
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:18.497
so as not to invent any measures here. Today we're going "by eye,"

37
00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:20.800
at least when it comes to me.

38
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:27.220
Now comes the moment where I'll need my hands. I'll still have to go to the store,

39
00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.200
I'm missing a few little things for the salad anyway.

40
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.464
So we'll do this, prepare the meat, and then I'll run out and buy the rest.

41
00:03:35.464 --> 00:03:40.661
Because I was thinking of making that coleslaw salad, if that's how it's even pronounced,

42
00:03:40.661 --> 00:03:43.900
but I think, yes. It will be an interesting addition.

43
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.289
To all of this I'll slice some onion,

44
00:03:47.289 --> 00:03:52.495
some tomatoes and maybe make that very tzatziki salad,

45
00:03:52.495 --> 00:03:56.400
so that everyone has something to combine to taste.

46
00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:05.050
Well, let's get to work, the flour will be literally just enough.

47
00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:13.700
Me and my "by eye" method when we start kneading.

48
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.602
Jokes aside, excellent. That is,

49
00:04:24.602 --> 00:04:33.100
you need to end up with a dough that doesn't stick too much to your hands. That's all.

50
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:41.509
Now I'll just wash my hands. Here I've cut pieces of baking paper,

51
00:04:41.509 --> 00:04:48.100
because I won't bake them right now, but will first shape them and set them aside,

52
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:54.892
to finish other tasks also related to making this recipe,

53
00:04:54.892 --> 00:05:01.500
and then we'll put them in to bake at the moment when I turn on the oven too.

54
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.123
To prepare the meat... "By eye" I make pieces, I don't know

55
00:05:07.123 --> 00:05:15.200
how many there'll be, but let's say let them be more or less the same.

56
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:29.800
And here's what's important for you: when you shape them, you can shape them by hand,

57
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.359
or with a rolling pin, it makes no difference at all.

58
00:05:33.359 --> 00:05:40.900
I always first flatten them a little with my hands to get a more or less right shape,

59
00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:47.995
and then go over the top with a rolling pin. But here, I'll show you that even that isn't necessary.

60
00:05:47.995 --> 00:05:55.000
This dough stretches very well, and if you lift it a bit into the air like this,

61
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:00.690
you get just the flatbread you need. I'll leave it like this,

62
00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:06.200
without the rolling pin this time, so they're a bit more rustic, I don't know.

63
00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:08.966
The rolling pin makes them very smooth,

64
00:06:08.966 --> 00:06:14.869
but I prefer it when the edges are a bit thicker. And now we just like this,

65
00:06:14.869 --> 00:06:19.800
I put baking paper on top so the next one doesn't stick so much.

66
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.818
And that's the whole philosophy of this part. I won't keep you here,

67
00:06:26.818 --> 00:06:31.100
because we'll need time to prepare the meat.

68
00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:35.813
It's somewhat different from anything you may have cooked up to now,

69
00:06:35.813 --> 00:06:37.556
and therefore more interesting,

70
00:06:37.556 --> 00:06:41.300
and this you've already seen on my channel, how I make it,

71
00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:47.501
so if you constantly watch recipes here, on the "Practično sa stilom" channel,

72
00:06:47.501 --> 00:06:52.600
all this is already familiar to you, so as not to repeat myself. I've made the flatbreads,

73
00:06:52.600 --> 00:07:03.600
I'll tidy the work surface and then get to the meat, but we'll move to that part of the kitchen over there.

74
00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:16.885
Now I'll move you here, not because we'll bake it here,

75
00:07:16.885 --> 00:07:20.846
but because I'll bring the blender to chop the onion and pepper,

76
00:07:20.846 --> 00:07:26.300
which are mixed with the ground meat along with the spices, and then spread on the baking tray.

77
00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.580
We go step by step.

78
00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:34.300
How sorry I am that this appliance is no longer for sale on the Lupa Maršala site.

79
00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:44.000
This EMA was the best choice to buy when it comes to simple food prep.

80
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:51.091
It serves me as a chopper, and blender, and everything, but it also has an attachment like a grater,

81
00:07:51.091 --> 00:07:56.900
which I'll need later for the salad, so I brought this attachment.

82
00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:03.374
Well, first we'll cut the onion and pepper. I took these two peppers, or rather,

83
00:08:03.374 --> 00:08:05.000
I couldn't find others.

84
00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:13.474
Here's our meat, let's take it out right away, we'll need it.

85
00:08:13.474 --> 00:08:18.400
And do I need anything else from here? I think not.

86
00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:34.800
And we cut the onion. I'll just cut it into large pieces, we'll remove this outer skin.

87
00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:43.570
Just into quarters. And this recipe is excellent. It's a sort of combination, say,

88
00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:46.900
of an Argentine ćevap, only that potato

89
00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:55.284
doesn't go inside, but these wonderful seasonings go in the form of fresh pepper,

90
00:08:55.284 --> 00:09:01.000
onion, garlic, and then we just season to taste.

91
00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:08.522
I cut everything into large pieces, because this chopper will chop it all.

92
00:09:08.522 --> 00:09:13.944
It turns into something like mush. I don't know how to explain it to you. That is, in no

93
00:09:13.944 --> 00:09:17.500
recipe will you feel pieces of anything.

94
00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:23.342
Maybe one or two more cloves of garlic, let's not overdo it,

95
00:09:23.342 --> 00:09:30.119
because the spreads will be very tasty. So there'll be enough of them, I think.

96
00:09:30.119 --> 00:09:34.500
Add two or three of these bigger ones? I think it'll be great.

97
00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:38.800
Basically, we turn it on. It'll get a bit harder now, so I won't talk.

98
00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:08.300
It literally turns into something like mush, which will now go into the meat.

99
00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:13.292
I took ground beef, you know how I love ground turkey,

100
00:10:13.292 --> 00:10:18.505
but there was no turkey: my delivery was postponed. I ordered,

101
00:10:18.505 --> 00:10:23.204
I'll be able to pick it up in two or three days, they informed me it's not available,

102
00:10:23.204 --> 00:10:25.700
so I had to improvise.

103
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.339
Anyway, there's a kilo of meat here, and what I'm putting in, look,

104
00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:34.616
is a portion for four or five people, considering that there are children too,

105
00:10:34.616 --> 00:10:36.600
I calculated it roughly that way.

106
00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:44.260
We add two eggs to the meat. We add about a hundred grams

107
00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:51.200
of some cheese, whether Trappist, mozzarella, any grated cheese.

108
00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:54.701
As for this mixture that I chopped up a bit,

109
00:10:54.701 --> 00:11:00.755
I put it in a colander to let it release water, considering that it has both onion and pepper,

110
00:11:00.755 --> 00:11:03.600
so the meat doesn't turn out too watery.

111
00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:13.600
I put it in a colander so it would release water, and now we add this chopped mixture right into the meat.

112
00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:26.374
What will we do next? We season this mixture. We do it like this: ground paprika,

113
00:11:26.374 --> 00:11:33.255
and I like to add "Vegeta," we'll add pepper,

114
00:11:33.255 --> 00:11:43.500
and after that all we have left is literally just to knead the mixture well by hand.

115
00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:52.556
I don't know whether I have Parmesan, Parmesan goes great here, if I have it,

116
00:11:52.556 --> 00:12:01.354
I'll add it, and you put a hundred grams of Parmesan in here and that's it, roll up your sleeves,

117
00:12:01.354 --> 00:12:06.400
and slowly the kneading begins.

118
00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:07.921
What's good:

119
00:12:07.921 --> 00:12:15.079
a mixture prepared this way can be formed into balls and put in the freezer.

120
00:12:15.079 --> 00:12:18.479
You freeze them, and when there's no time,

121
00:12:18.479 --> 00:12:23.400
you take out the meatballs, just thaw them and bake.

122
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:31.128
I keep such a mixture in the freezer for about three to six months, and nothing happens to it.

123
00:12:31.128 --> 00:12:38.431
So know this: since you're cooking anyway, if there's any extra, freeze it without worry, it comes out very,

124
00:12:38.431 --> 00:12:39.300
very well.

125
00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:42.500
This is what the mixture looks like now.

126
00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:51.500
We'll take one large baking tray from the oven, which you'll line with baking paper.

127
00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:04.600
And now you take this ground meat and literally spread it on the surface.

128
00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:12.891
You can do this part by hand without worry, or maybe with a glass or a rolling pin,

129
00:13:12.891 --> 00:13:14.500
and stretch it out like this.

130
00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:26.100
From edge to edge of the tray, stretch it so everything is roughly the same thickness.

131
00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:32.000
You can wet your hands a bit if that's more convenient.

132
00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:40.155
Good. Now that we've done this step, take this spatula,

133
00:13:40.155 --> 00:13:46.985
and what do we do? We form such even corners. I'll start here now,

134
00:13:46.985 --> 00:13:53.000
and then I'll move closer so you can see well.

135
00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:01.849
Now you do this to the end of the tray. And after that you literally cut it like this,

136
00:14:01.849 --> 00:14:05.900
so that it comes out roughly like one kebab.

137
00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:10.996
That is, you can take this part. When it's baked,

138
00:14:10.996 --> 00:14:14.766
it'll then be additionally shaped.

139
00:14:14.766 --> 00:14:19.500
You take it and put it in the homemade tortilla you've made.

140
00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:28.400
And now we go across like this, following the line we started a bit earlier. And there.

141
00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:37.266
And now you basically send it into the oven like this. It bakes for about forty minutes. Until you see

142
00:14:37.266 --> 00:14:44.200
that it has baked nicely, so that no raw meat is left inside.

143
00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:48.387
And then you just take out these strips and arrange them on a tray.

144
00:14:48.387 --> 00:14:52.500
I sincerely hope it turns out the way I imagined.

145
00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:59.131
I won't put this in to bake now. I'm going to deal with the salad,

146
00:14:59.131 --> 00:15:03.600
and before they arrive we'll put this in the oven so it's at least a little warm.

147
00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:08.928
But in any case I'm taking you through the entire process, however long it takes, several hours,

148
00:15:08.928 --> 00:15:12.100
considering that it's now two o'clock and the guests arrive at six.

149
00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:17.599
I'm making coleslaw: I'll try to grate three carrots and the cabbage. I don't know,

150
00:15:17.599 --> 00:15:20.986
I'll grate the carrot, that is, it has to be very fine,

151
00:15:20.986 --> 00:15:22.800
and the cabbage I might slice.

152
00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:29.161
And then I mix with a small amount of lemon juice, vinegar, salt, pepper, sugar and

153
00:15:29.161 --> 00:15:33.900
mayonnaise, and you get such a nice little salad, which will be a side dish.

154
00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:38.000
So now you won't hear me because of this noise, so I decided to do the intro before this.

155
00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:54.508
And why do I love this appliance? And why am I sad it's no longer around? Here's why:

156
00:15:54.508 --> 00:15:59.500
how it slices this carrot in three and a half seconds.

157
00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:06.020
You know, if I sliced and grated like that, never. The cabbage I'll still cut by hand,

158
00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:10.867
because I believe the appliance will turn it into mush,

159
00:16:10.867 --> 00:16:12.900
so we chop by hand.

160
00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:19.900
We cut the cabbage. This one is just small, nice, convenient.

161
00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:27.395
I don't know how much you can see. I cut as finely as possible, I'm not getting out the grater now,

162
00:16:27.395 --> 00:16:30.111
because then I'd have to wash it too.

163
00:16:30.111 --> 00:16:36.200
And so this lunch will have seven hundred and one dirty bowls, a baking tray and the like.

164
00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:46.803
Now like this, then once more crosswise, I think it'll be great.

165
00:16:46.803 --> 00:16:50.100
And now like this, even finer.

166
00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:09.941
I put it in this bowl with the carrot and mix it. And now I'm right here,

167
00:17:09.941 --> 00:17:14.900
so as not to dirty another seven hundred and one dishes.

168
00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:24.682
Now we'll prepare this mixture here too. That is, we add mayonnaise,

169
00:17:24.682 --> 00:17:36.200
we add a bit of lemon juice to the salad, by the way, I often get this one at KFC.

170
00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:47.233
We add two teaspoons of sugar, add a teaspoon of salt, pepper to taste.

171
00:17:47.233 --> 00:17:53.100
As much as you like to be noticeable, add that much.

172
00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:58.048
We're classic, so about half a teaspoon.

173
00:17:58.048 --> 00:18:02.155
And I have to add a bit more apple cider vinegar.

174
00:18:02.155 --> 00:18:06.100
And maybe like this, one capful of apple cider vinegar.

175
00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:12.600
Now I'll mix and taste, and we'll see whether anything else needs adding.

176
00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:26.447
Over-salted. I'm missing onion here. I didn't find any onion going in it,

177
00:18:26.447 --> 00:18:30.500
but I'd add a little onion here too.

178
00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:41.700
Onion isn't mandatory, but you can add a little green onion. I think it'll be much, much better with it.

179
00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:51.400
Let's not overdo it, just a little for flavor. Literally. Like this. This one little stalk.

180
00:18:55.300 --> 00:19:03.690
I remember that at KFC there's onion in it when I read the ingredients. So

181
00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:08.500
we'll trust my taste, not the recipe.

182
00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:20.200
You see what it looks like. Now I'll taste, let's see whether we changed anything.

183
00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:31.800
Much better. But still salty. Maybe add a little mayonnaise to soften it.

184
00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:45.200
There, into this. And now it goes in the fridge to chill well.

185
00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:50.939
So, it's time to bake our tortillas. A heated

186
00:19:50.939 --> 00:19:57.800
pan with a little oil, and we simply lay them in.

187
00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:06.734
Now I'll find that flipping thing, to make it easier. A bit of patience,

188
00:20:06.734 --> 00:20:12.100
a strong back and a good mood — and you have ready tortillas.

189
00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:16.295
I've already filmed this, I don't think you'll find it interesting,

190
00:20:16.295 --> 00:20:20.299
but in any case a couple of seconds on one side and the other, depending on

191
00:20:20.299 --> 00:20:22.396
how hot the pan is, you'll see

192
00:20:22.396 --> 00:20:26.400
that at some point they puff up a bit and stop, and that's it.

193
00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:30.276
So there, you've followed everything, how good this one is.

